# Committed readings for udp.wasm: wasi:sockets@0.3's whole udp-socket resource at the ABI (#1376 E3, ADR 0140).
# Built:       wasm-tools parse udp.wat -o udp.wasm   (wasm-tools 1.258.0; validates --features all)
#              sha256 c0ef453887082c8a816b4eb154a9b53666519b0ec1d42c6f7b6e4807895a25b5, 9838 bytes
# Invocation:  .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime run -W component-model-async=y -W component-model-more-async-builtins=y \
#                -S p3=y -S inherit-network --invoke 'run(<row>)' udp.wasm
#              (component-model-more-async-builtins for the async subtask.cancel; the `denied` row has no -S
#              inherit-network)
# Peer:        none; every socket is the row's own, on loopback.
# Engine:      wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), darwin/arm64. Runs: 10 of 10 per line, each identical. Taken
#              2026-11-28, before the engine code.
#
# Each line is `<row>: <reading>`, and the test reads them by row. The row's number is its line's position, from 0,
# counting row lines only, with `park` (40) a harness row that has no line. A reading of `trap` is a run that traps,
# wasmtime's message given after it. Four bytes, byte 0 the low one; an operation's byte is 0 for ok and 0x80 | its
# error-code case (0x80 access-denied, 0x82 invalid-argument, 0x85 invalid-state, 0x86 address-not-bindable, 0x87
# address-in-use, 0x89 connection-refused, 0x8d datagram-too-large); a property's byte is 0 or 1. Each row's four
# bytes are named in its comment in udp.wat.
#
# **Rows marked darwin below read the kernel, not the engine**: Burroughs asks the kernel as wasmtime does, so on
# another kernel the answer is that kernel's, and the test compares these rows on darwin only (ADR 0140).
bind-v4: 16842752
bind-v6: 16842752
fresh-getters: 16811397
bind-refusals: 2189591808
bind-more: 8881794
sendto-v4: 16842752
sendto-v6: 16842752
connect-send: 0
connect-filter: 0
send-none: 33410
send-mismatch: 33280
send-implicit: 65536
connect-implicit: 65536
receive-unbound: 133
disconnect: 2189788416
disconnect-unconnected: 34181
# darwin: its udp_disconnect resets the local address to the unspecified one, so byte 2 (still 127.0.0.1) is 0. By its
# source Linux keeps an address a bind chose; unmeasured.
disconnect-local: 16777216
connect-refusals: 2189591170
reconnect: 0
empty: 16842752
big: 16842752
# The ipv6 pair (bytes 0 and 1) is refused by the host's 65535-byte bound, not the kernel's: darwin's kernel takes a
# 65536-byte ipv6 datagram from a socket with a 1 MiB send buffer, measured with Go's net.
sizes-64k: 2374864269
# darwin: a datagram larger than the receive buffer is dropped, so the 4 bytes are what arrives. By its source Linux
# queues the first datagram whatever its size; unmeasured.
rcvbuf-small: 16777216
# Not asked on Windows, where Go's net disables SIO_UDP_CONNRESET, so no ICMP error is ever surfaced.
refused: 8978432
hop-limit: 16810496
# darwin: the defaults, 64 for ipv4 and 255 for ipv6 (IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS is -1, read as a u8). Other kernels unmeasured.
hop-defaults: 4294918208
hop-limit-v6: 2181103616
rbuf: 33280
sbuf: 33280
buf-huge: 0
options-carry: 0
#
# Burroughs', not wasmtime's, by ADR 0140's cancel: a cancel of a pending receive resolves at the cancel, so the async
# subtask.cancel returns CANCELLED_BEFORE_RETURNED (4) itself. wasmtime 49.0.3 returns BLOCKED and then resolves the
# subtask CANCELLED_BEFORE_RETURNED, and reads 5121 (0x00001401): STARTED; 0x10 | 4, the cancel blocked and then
# resolved; the send ok; the next receive takes the datagram and B drops cleanly.
cancel: 1025
#
blocked: 16777217
sync-lowers: 16842752
two-datagrams: 65536
dial-v4: 0
dial-v6: 0
send-refusals: 2189591170
broadcast: 32768
# Read with no network flag: create of either family is access-denied.
denied: 32896
max-v4: 16813312
max-v6: 16842752
send-oob: trap: list pointer/length out of bounds of memory
connect-any: 16842752
disconnect-receive: 16777216
queued-status: 16777472
#
# Burroughs', not wasmtime's, by ADR 0140's connected filter and the WIT's text ("receive will only return datagrams
# sent from the provided remote-address"): a datagram from C queued before B's connect is not B's to receive, so B's
# receive is A's datagram. wasmtime 49.0.3 returns C's, reading 16777216 (0x01000000): the sends ok; the receive ok;
# not from A; from C. That reading is the row as committed, whose drain through D makes it 10 of 10; the row's first
# draft, without the drain, read from-A 9 of 10 and from-C 1 of 10, a race with darwin's loopback queue.
connect-after-arrival: 65536
#
# darwin: its default send buffer is net.inet.udp.maxdgram, 9216 bytes, so 9217 is datagram-too-large.
kernel-maxdgram: 36096
# darwin: after the disconnect the local address is the unspecified one with the bound port kept. By its source Linux
# unbinds a port nobody chose; unmeasured.
disconnect-any: 16842752
